Document Chunking Without Overlap (LangChain RAG Pipeline)
This snippet demonstrates the initial text-splitting phase of a semantic Retrieval-Augmented Generation with LangChain preprocessing pipeline using RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter.

In unstructured document workflows (such as processing long-form podcast transcripts, research reports, or PDFs), documents are often too large to fit within an LLM's context window or too granular to yield precise semantic search results. This script breaks down raw documents into smaller, uniform segments while deliberately setting the chunk overlap to zero to highlight why boundary management matters.

Prerequisites & Dependencies
Python 3.8+

langchain-text-splitters library

Install the required package via pip:

Bash
pip install langchain-text-splitters
Code Overview
Python
from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter

# Initialize the text splitter with a 1000-character limit and 0 overlap
text_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=1000, chunk_overlap=0)

# Split the loaded documents into chunks
all_splits = text_splitter.split_documents(docs)

# Output metrics and boundary samples
print(f"Created {len(all_splits)} chunks without overlap")
print(f"\nEnd of first chunk: ...{all_splits[0].page_content[-250:]}")
print(f"\nStart of second chunk: {all_splits[1].page_content[:250]}...")
How It Works
RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter: This splitter attempts to split text recursively along natural boundaries (such as paragraphs, newlines, sentences, and words) until each chunk satisfies the target size constraints.

chunk_size=1000: Restricts the maximum length of any single text chunk to 1,000 characters.

chunk_overlap=0: Disables sliding-window overlap between adjacent chunks.

Note on trade-offs: While setting overlap to zero ensures no redundant text is stored, it frequently results in sentences or thoughts getting abruptly cut off at boundaries (e.g., ending a chunk mid-sentence), which can occasionally obscure context during vector similarity retrieval.

